Molluscum Contagiosum
Young Adult & Adolecence
Pearly dome-shaped papules, Central umbilication, Clustered distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-029
Reason for Visit: Acute psychosocial distress, anxiety, and sleep disruption following the rapid spread of previously unnoticed genital and lower truncal lesions.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient in an acute state of psychological panic regarding newly discovered, rapidly spreading papules in the genital and lower abdominal region, fearing a severe, incurable sexually transmitted infection. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was required to definitively diagnose the lesions visually, exclude actively ulcerating or painful pathology (e.g., herpes simplex), and provide immediate clinical reassurance to de-escalate the acute psychological crisis prior to routine GP hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have these weird, shiny bumps spreading all over my stomach and groin, and I am terrified it's herpes or something permanent."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 4-week history of a progressively worsening, asymptomatic skin eruption. The lesions began as a few isolated spots on the lower abdomen and have since multiplied, forming a clustered distribution on the trunk and extending into the intertriginous areas and genitals. The patient describes them as firm, dome-shaped, flesh-coloured to pearly papules. They are typically painless but occasionally pruritic. The patient admits to scratching them, noting that this autoinoculation has led to linear arrays of new papules. The acute distress tonight was triggered by the patient noticing the rapid spread and catastrophizing about the etiology, resulting in a severe acute anxiety episode and inability to sleep.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly distracted, anxious, and fixated on the lesions.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety regarding sexual health; risk of aggressive, self-inflicted skin damage from picking at the lesions.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, pacing, and seeking immediate, definitive reassurance regarding the visual findings.
Physical Exam: Dermatological and genitourinary examination (chaperoned) reveals multiple 2-5mm firm, pearly, dome-shaped papules. Crucially, close inspection confirms a distinct central umbilication (a small dimple) within the lesions. They are arranged in a clustered distribution across the suprapubic region, lower abdomen, and proximal medial thighs. Several lesions exhibit a surrounding, mild eczematous reaction. There are no painful vesicles, no large exophytic cauliflower-like growths, and no tender lymphadenopathy. The clinical appearance firmly matches the diagnostic tetrad of pearly dome-shaped papules, central umbilication, and a clustered distribution, allowing for confident clinical diagnosis without the need for biopsy to identify Henderson-Paterson bodies.
Environmental Observations: Patient has a loofah and harsh body scrubs in the shower, which are actively spreading the viral particles via micro-abrasions.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Molluscum Contagiosum - Status: Stable, Acute Psychosocial Distress
Differentials:

Genital Herpes (HSV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lesions being firm, painless, umbilicated papules rather than exquisitely painful, fluid-filled vesicles or shallow, tender ulcers.

Genital Warts (HPV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of central umbilication and the smooth, pearly appearance, entirely lacking the exophytic, verrucous, cauliflower-like morphology of warts.

Folliculitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lesions lacking a central hair follicle or purulent pustular head, and possessing the classic pearly, umbilicated viral structure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of acute psychological panic driven by the discovery and rapid spread of anogenital lesions, misinterpreting them as a severe, painful, or permanent sexually transmitted disease. The immediate after-hours home visit was clinically justified by the necessity to de-escalate a severe psychosocial crisis and definitively rule out highly infectious ulcerative pathology (e.g., herpes). Direct clinical visualization immediately confirmed classic, benign, self-limiting Molluscum Contagiosum. Delaying the assessment would have resulted in profound, unmanaged distress and potential destructive self-treatment (picking/popping). The immediate provision of a definitive, benign diagnosis instantly resolved the acute anxiety.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided immediate, factual clinical reassurance regarding the benign, superficial, and entirely self-limiting viral nature of the condition. Advised the patient to completely cease all picking, scratching, or popping of the lesions. Recommended a standard STI screen via the regular GP as routine protocol for any new genital presentation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately halt the spread of the virus across your body, completely stop using loofahs, sponges, or shared towels, acknowledging that these items actively trap the virus and drag it into tiny microscopic cuts in healthy skin. To prevent autoinoculation, purposefully keep your hands away from the lesions; if you must touch them, wash your hands thoroughly with soap immediately after, creating a hard physical barrier against viral spread.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to monitor for spontaneous resolution or to discuss destructive therapies (e.g., cryotherapy) if the lesions become cosmetically or psychologically intolerable.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if a lesion becomes rapidly swollen, bright red, hot, and begins leaking thick yellow pus, indicating a severe secondary bacterial infection, or if a high fever develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is based on the classic clinical appearance; the condition is benign but highly contagious to direct skin contact.
Clinical Course: These viral bumps are entirely harmless and will eventually undergo spontaneous resolution occurring over months to years as your immune system recognizes and destroys the virus.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if the skin around any of the bumps turns bright red, becomes hot to the touch, and starts throbbing, as this means a secondary bacterial infection has occurred.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: These bumps are caused by a very mild, superficial skin virus. They are not herpes, they do not cause pain, and they will not damage your body. If you apply lateral pressure, they extrude a caseous core full of the virus, which is why picking at them instantly spreads the infection to nearby skin.
Actionable Steps: Stop shaving the affected area immediately to prevent micro-cuts that spread the virus. Wash the area gently with your hands only, and pat dry with a clean towel that is washed after every single use.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Molluscum Contagiosum (Benign Skin Virus). Not dangerous. Stop shaving or scratching the area to prevent spread. See regular GP in 4 weeks if you wish to discuss removal.

SMS EDUCATION

Do not pop or pick the bumps; this spreads the virus. Stop using loofahs and wash gently with hands only. The condition is harmless and will resolve naturally over time.
